1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,948
Perception models need thousands of labeled images.

2
00:00:03,948 --> 00:00:10,167
In this module Isaac Sim produces them for you: a warehouse full of randomized pallet jacks,

3
00:00:10,167 --> 00:00:12,804
written out as a labeled KITTI dataset.

4
00:00:13,704 --> 00:00:16,347
The idea is called domain randomization.

5
00:00:16,347 --> 00:00:20,576
You vary everything that should not matter: where the camera is,

6
00:00:20,576 --> 00:00:25,201
how objects are placed and colored, the lights and the floor textures.

7
00:00:25,201 --> 00:00:28,505
The object you care about, the pallet jack, stays.

8
00:00:28,505 --> 00:00:33,197
So the model learns the object itself rather than one particular scene,

9
00:00:33,197 --> 00:00:36,699
which narrows the gap between simulation and reality.

10
00:00:36,699 --> 00:00:42,778
And because the renderer knows where everything is, every bounding box label comes for free.

11
00:00:43,678 --> 00:00:45,251
Here is the pipeline.

12
00:00:45,251 --> 00:00:50,419
Load the warehouse, add six pallet jacks and twelve distractor props,

13
00:00:50,419 --> 00:00:56,261
keep labels only on the pallet jacks, and attach a camera with a KITTI writer.

14
00:00:56,261 --> 00:01:03,975
Then, for every frame, randomize, step the orchestrator, and the writer saves the image and its labels.

15
00:01:04,875 --> 00:01:08,672
The course script does not work unchanged in six point one.

16
00:01:08,672 --> 00:01:12,985
On this build, the graph randomizers fired only on the first frame,

17
00:01:12,985 --> 00:01:18,713
so every image was identical, and the material randomizer hung for more than ten minutes.

18
00:01:18,713 --> 00:01:21,351
The port uses the functional API instead,

19
00:01:21,351 --> 00:01:24,569
which is also what the six point one examples use,

20
00:01:24,569 --> 00:01:28,882
and it blocks the warehouse labels that come from referenced files.

21
00:01:29,782 --> 00:01:31,496
Running it is one command.

22
00:01:31,496 --> 00:01:35,584
For a quick look, run the m3 pallet jack script with a window.

23
00:01:35,584 --> 00:01:37,100
It makes twenty frames.

24
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:41,254
For the real dataset, run it headless with two thousand frames.

25
00:01:41,254 --> 00:01:45,738
The images and KITTI labels land in the output folder, under Camera.

26
00:01:45,738 --> 00:01:48,903
Training the detector happens outside Isaac Sim,

27
00:01:48,903 --> 00:01:54,441
with the course repository's notebooks, and nothing there changes for six point one.

